    "body": "Brixton Metal's camp at the Langis project on the shore of Lake Temiskaming in northeast Ontario. Credit: Brixton Metals.\nPosted By: Northern Miner Staff November 16, 2026\nBrixton Metals (TSXV: BBB) has drilled its highest silver grade yet at the Langis project in Ontario, finding shallow veins in a new target area as it works towards an initial resource estimate. The stock surged.\nHole LM-26-477 cut 3 metres at 14,559 grams silver per tonne from 68.5 metres depth, including 0.3 metre at 142,770 grams, Brixton said Monday . A follow-up hole cut 5 metres at 704 grams from 67 metres.\nThe narrower interval was “the highest-grade silver intercept ever drilled on the project,” chairman and CEO Gary Thompson said.\nBrixton shares surged 10% to 55¢ Monday afternoon in Toronto, valuing the company at about $53 million (US$37 million). The stock has ranged between 45¢ and $1.45 over the past year.\nShort intervals\nThe results add a shallow target to a property with a long production history, but the short intervals require care: true widths are unknown, and further drilling must establish continuity. The headline assay measures silver concentration in a small section of core, rather than the size of a deposit.\nLangis lies about 500 km north of Toronto in the Cobalt district, within the traditional territory of Timiskaming First Nation. Highway 65 crosses the property, which has access to power and rail infrastructure.\nThe new Shaft 4 target contains silver in the upper part of a conglomerate rock unit. Most known mineralization lies near its lower contact, giving Brixton another horizon to test beneath the shallow veins.\nJuly intercept\nThe discovery follows a wider intersection announced in July at Shaft 6 Southeast, where hole LM-26-377 cut 20 metres at 5,015 grams silver per tonne from 165 metres depth. That result included 14 metres at 7,150 grams.\nThe former Langis mine produced more than 10.4 million oz. of silver and about 358,000 lb. of cobalt, according to Brixton. Its workings extend roughly 150 metres below surface and are flooded.\nAlthough historical production establishes the district’s mining pedigree, it doesn’t establish a current resource for Brixton’s new targets.\nExploration funding\nBrixton closed the final portion of a $7.16-million (US$5.02-million) flow-through share financing Friday. The proceeds are intended mainly for exploration and drilling at Langis and the Thorn copper-gold project in British Columbia.\nThe financing involved about 10.2 million shares across three closings. Flow-through financing allows qualifying exploration expenditures to be passed through to investors for tax purposes, directing the money towards exploration rather than general corporate spending. Print\nTAG: Exploration Ontario Silver\nCOMMODITY: Silver\nCOMPANY: Brixton Metals\nRepublish this article",
